('h1', '8. Search, priority and reproducibility'),
('search_table', ''),
('p', 'The scout has 2,048 Sobol settings plus three controls, seed 2026110181, damping 0.001–0.999 and relative tolerance 10<super>-12</super>–10<super>-1</super>. It measures 50,000 steps after 20,000 warm-up. Every flag receives a fresh million-step follow-up with 100,000 warm-up. D802 exits the solution cutoff 10<super>10</super> during the scout warm-up; this is an observed cutoff exit, not a divergence theorem. Regular, weak and failed outcomes are preserved.'),
('p', 'The packet retains six five-million-step checks, eight independent precision checks, four actual-library trajectories, 252 start/parameter controls, 65,536 volume trajectories, both damping slices and exact figure inputs. In addition to the original 24 gallery clouds, all 13 million-step cases with both leading directions above 0.001 in every window receive retained portraits. A pre-execution syntax error and a conservative symbolic draft are recorded. Duplicate blocks are compared with their aggregates before omission. Two large raw portraits stay local with hashes and regeneration information; exact histograms and animation tails are public.'),
('h2', 'Prior work and the narrow unresolved question'),
('p', 'Hall and Higham [3] analyze coupled solution/step recurrences and practical norm dependence. Higham and Hall [4] already study the Dormand–Prince family, equilibrium stability and complex-dominant linear examples; all nine pages were inspected. Higham, Humphries and Wain [5] discuss tolerance-scale oscillation near stable physical equilibria. Ranocha and Giesselmann [6] develop related step-control stability analysis. Those phenomena and that framework are not our discoveries.'),
('p', 'The candidate contribution is a reproducible characterization of these specific geometries and finite two-direction regimes, with exact accepted-solution bounds. The bounded review did not locate an exact published match. That absence is not a priority certificate. Hall (1986), Wain\'s 1998 thesis, Söderlind (2002), broader numerical-dynamics books and equivalent reduced maps remain material coverage gaps. The online audit also credits older spurious-solution and cosymmetric numerical-chaos literature, distinguishing abstract-only from full-text inspection.'),
('p', 'This companion study extends the repository\'s RK23 investigation using the higher-order existing pair; it does not count changing the pair or parameters as an independent mechanism discovery. Proving asymptotic attraction and comparing invariant-set structure across switching surfaces remain open. **Historical uniqueness remains unresolved.**'.replace('**','')),
